                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I finally got around to finishing *Call of Cthulhu: Prisoner of Ice*, a game I only played briefly as a kid and never managed to complete. After all these years, I decided to buy it and experience it from start to finish, and I'm really glad I did.\r\n\r\nThis is a classic Lovecraft-inspired point-and-click adventure with a fantastic atmosphere. The story is excellent, the graphics are gorgeous in that unmistakable mid-90s style, the dialogue is engaging, and the puzzles strike a nice balance between challenging and logical.\r\n\r\nIf I had one criticism, it's that I found the first half more compelling than the second. The later locations are much smaller, which means there are fewer objects and interactions to experiment with, making the exploration feel a bit more limited. The timed sequences also rely more on trial and error than on actual puzzle-solving, so they can be a little frustrating.\r\n\r\nThat said, the second half is also where the plot really takes off. The story becomes increasingly intricate, with some clever time paradoxes that fit the Lovecraftian narrative surprisingly well. I also loved that the game offers two different endings, giving you a reason to see how events can unfold differently.\r\n\r\nOverall, Call of Cthulhu: Prisoner of Ice is a wonderful adventure game that has aged remarkably well if you appreciate classic point-and-clicks. It was a nostalgic trip for me, but even without the nostalgia I think it's a genuinely enjoyable experience. Highly recommended.\r\n",
